## 1. Introduction
The intermittent depletion of non-renewable energy has strengthened the demand for efficient energy storage devices [1–3]. Researchers are turning attention to various energy storage devices, such as supercapacitors, rechargeable batteries and fuel cells. Supercapacitors stand out between the traditional capacitor and the rechargeable battery with the merit of their ultra-high power density, short charge and discharge period and long cycling life [4–6]. To develop a supercapacitor with good performance, it is a crucial step to design the electrode materials. According to the mechanisms of charge storage, the electrode materials can be classified into two categories, which are separately electrical doublelayer capacitors (EDLCs) and pseudocapacitor materials [7,8]. Especially, owing to the reversible multi-electron surface faradaic redox reactions on the pseudocapacitor electrode materials, the specific capacitance and power density of the pseudocapacitors are more prominent than that of EDLCs [9,10]. Inevitably, the design and fabrication of superior pseudocapacitor materials have become hot topics recently [11–13].
MOFs are a class of porous materials formed by self-assembly of metal ions and organic ligands [14], which have received considerable attention because of their favorable electrical conductivity and rich valences, and have been regarded as a promising candidate for pseudocapacitor materials [15,16]. The MOFs subclass materials can be integrated with various nanomaterials or polymers to improve the performance of electrode materials [17,18]. As a typical MOF, Zeolitic imidazole framework (ZIF) is a suitable template for constructing hollow structure with huge specific surface area, superior electrical conductivity, hierarchical pore size and redox activity, and its development greatly promote the process of developing high-performance supercapacitors [19–21]. For example, ZIF-67 crystals can be converted into cobalt sulfide $(\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8})$ as electrode materials by a simple hydrothermal method. $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ owns an ultra-high theoretical specific capacitance (5449 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ) [22]. However, the actual specific capacity of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ is always lower than the theoretical specific capacity and the utilization rate of the electrode material is not satisfactory. Some notable efforts have been attempted. For example, some researchers have made attempts to integrate rGO and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ through many approaches, which reveals the advantages in a specific capacity of $808\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}~}$ at a $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}s^{-1}$ scan rate [23]. Xiling Mao [24] et al. fabricated a novel nanoparticle $(\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}@\mathsf{S}_{-\Gamma\mathsf{G}0})$ and delivered a specific capacitance of $348.45\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . A kind of $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ nanoparticle-decorated carbon nanofibers reported by Zhang et al. exhibit desired capacitance of $718\mathrm{F}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ [25]. Those works are meaningful and greatly deliver superior electrochemical performance. However, the specific capacitance and cycle life are not remarkable, which restricts its practical application in high-performance supercapacitors.
To overcome the above limitations, it remains a great challenge to enhance the actual specific capacitance and cycling stability. Such as, it can be integrated with other different nanomaterials and nanopolymers [26]. Currently, transition metal oxides exhibit versatile application in pseudocapacitor combining the merit with high reversible faradaic redox reactions and high energy density. Such as $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ [27], $\mathsf{M o o}_{3}$ [28], $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ [29], and CoO [30] have attracted considerable attention used as electrode materials for supercapacitors. Nonetheless, compared with various transition metal oxides, NiO endows with a variety of oxidation states and structures, which can ensure abundant redox reactions on the surface of the electrodes and enhance superior specific capacitance [31–33]. Theoretically, a high power density of electrode materials can only be achieved via fast electron and ion transport, and the electrode materials integrated with NiO are considered to store and deliver excellent specific capacitance with long cycling stability [34,35]. Inspired by the above considerations, we aimed at combing the advantages of homogeneous hollow structures and shortening the length of electrolyte ions migration, to increase the utilization rate of the electrode material and obtain good electrochemical properties.
Herein, we designed and controlled the synthesis of metal cobalt-based compounds by using ZIF-67 as a precursor, a hierarchical core-shell $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ structure was synthesized by the introduction of NiO. A systematic investigation was performed to determine the micromorphology and structure of the prepared materials, its electrochemical performance as a supercapacitor electrode material was studied as well. As expected, the coated of NiO can lead to a superior electrochemical performance, which offers a large specific surface between the electrode and electrolyte, and provides a short path for ion migration as well as improves the utilization rate of the electrode material. It contributes an excellent electrochemical performance and outlooks that $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ was a promising candidate for potential applications in the energy storage field.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
The fabrication procedure of the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ composite is shown in Fig. 1. The reagents used in this study: Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $99\%$ , $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}^{\cdot}$ , 2-methylimidazole $(98\%)$ , nickel nitrate hexahydrate $98\%$ , $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ , thioacetamide $(99\%$ ， TAA), methanol $(99.99\%)$ , and urea $(\geq99.5\%)$ were all obtained from Alading Co., Ltd. All above reagents and materials were used as received.
## 2.2. Preparation of $C o_{9}S_{8}@N i O$ nanocomposites
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of ZIF-67
A traditional facile precipitation reaction was used to prepare the metal-organic framework (ZIF-67) [36]. Briefly, $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(5.238\mathrm{g})$ , 2-methylimidazole $(3.955\mathrm{g})$ were respectively dissolved in methanol solution $(200~\mathrm{mL}$ with stirred for five minutes and formed C solution and M solution. Next, pour the M solution into the C solution and stir for three minutes. After this, the mixed solution was aged for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature. Then the resulting precipitate was collected by centrifugalizing with ethanol and deionized (DI) water in turn, finally, dried overnight in $60^{\circ}C$ vacuum to obtain ZIF-67.
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of $C o_{9}S_{8}$
In a typical synthesis, as-synthesized ZIF-67 $(0.20\:\mathrm{g})$ was added to ethanol solution and stirred. Then, $0.6\ {\tt g}$ TAA was put into the dispersion solution and stirred uniformly. Follow by this procedure, the mixture was poured into a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave for $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ . This process was allowed cooling down to room temperature and collected, then products $\phantom{+}C09S_{8}$ were obtained by wash with ethanol and water with the ratio of (1:1) for six times, drying under a $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ vacuum overnight.
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of $C o_{9}S_{8}@N i O$
## 2.3. Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD Rigaku, Cu-Ka) was utilized to investigate the crystalline property of the samples. X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) was used to characterize surface elements. The analysis was performed under a power of 150 W with the aluminum target $(\mathsf{A l~\ K\alpha~\ h\upsilon}~=~1486.6~\mathsf{e v})$ as $\mathsf{X}$ -ray source. The microstructure of the samples was measured by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI Verios G4, USA) and field emission transmission electron microscope (FETEM, FEI Talos F200X TEM). Energy dispersive spectra (EDS) for elemental mapping analysis were characterized by a FETEM microscope. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET, Belsorp-max) were used to measure the specific surface area and pore size distribution.
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
For the three-electrode system tests, the electrochemical measurements of cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic chargedischarge (GCD), and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were performed on an electrochemical workstation (Wuhan CorrTest, China). In special, the CV was measured under the voltage window of 0–0.47 V from 2 to $100\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ scan rates, the GCD was recorded at different current densities between 0 and $0.47\mathrm{~V~}$ , the EIS was observed in the frequency range from 0.01 to $100~\mathrm{kHZ}$ with potential amplitude of $5\mathrm{mV}$ . For the working electrode, at a weight ratio of 80:10:10, the mixing active material, polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) and acetylene black construct it. The appropriate amount of $N\mathrm{.}$ -methyl pyrrolidone (NMP) was added and stirred to form slurry, which was then smeared on precleaned nickel foam $(1~\mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 1~\mathrm{cm})$ under $60~^{\circ}C$ overnight drying. Subsequently, working electrodes were fabricated under a pressure of $10\ \mathrm{MPa}$ . The electrochemical measurements of individual working electrodes were conducted in 6 M KOH aqueous solution as electrolyte. Pt foil electrode and $\mathrm{\sfHg/HgO}$ electrode perform as counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively.
For the two-electrode system tests, the asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC) $(\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}@\mathsf{N i O}//\mathsf{A C})$ was assembled by taking the active material, AC, and 6 M KOH as cathode electrode, anode electrode, and electrolyte, respectively. The loading mass of the cathode and anode materials were matched precisely by the charge balance mechanism $\mathbf{\partial}Q^{+}=Q^{-}$ , $\begin{array}{r}{Q=C_{S}\times m\Delta V,}\end{array}$ ), where $C_{S}$ and DVare specific capacitances and potential windows of positive and negative materials. Then, the specific capacitances $\left(\mathsf C,\mathsf{F}\thinspace\mathsf{g}^{-1}\right)$ ), power density (P, W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}.$ ), and energy density (E, Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) were calculated as following equations:
$$
C=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{m\times\Delta V}
$$
$$
P=\frac{3600\times E}{\Delta t}
$$
$$
E=\frac{C\times\Delta V^{2}}{7.2}
$$
where I (A) is the discharge current, $\Delta t$ (s) is the discharge time; $m$ (g) and $\Delta V$ (V) are the mass of active materials and potential window, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of morphology and structure
Fig. 2 gives the SEM images of the as-synthesized samples. The morphology of ZIF-67 displays the diamond dodecahedron structure with a smooth surface (Fig. S1a). ZIF-67 composite was eventually converted into CoSx polyhedral structure when putting TAA as a sulfur source. Subsequently, $S^{2-}$ osmosis the core from the surface and react with the $C o^{2+}$ accomplished the sulfurization procedure, accompanied with the dissolve of ZIF-67 and as a result of hollow nanostructure [37,38]. Compared with ZIF-67, the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ is relatively rough, still maintains the typical and uniform polyhedron morphology structure of the original ZIF-67 template (Fig. 2a). Specifically, the cavity structure of $\phantom{+}C09S_{8}$ is confirmed by the accidentally ruptured. It can be found from Fig. 2d that the morphology of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ shows a uniform and stacking nanosheet structure with good dispersibility. Furthermore, the SEM and EDS elemental mapping of NiO display the fact that the NiO possesses a nanosheet structure and the even distribution of Ni and O elements (Figs. S1c-1f). In detail, the interlaced NiO nanosheets distribute on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ uniformly and closely, such a network structure is beneficial to transport electrons and ions, increasing the number of electrochemical active sites and capacitance performance (Fig. 2e). Additionally, the volume effect of the redox reaction can be reduced by the core-shell structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{Ni}0$ and enhance the stability of the material. Also, from the EDS elemental mapping of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ (Fig. 2b), the results shown the uniform distribution of Co and S elements, demonstrating the generation of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ successfully.
On the basis of the above distinction, more information of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ were further analyzed by TEM and HRTEM (Fig. 2c, and Fig. 2f 2h). The TEM images of the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ composites are shown in Fig. 2c and Fig. 2f. Fig. 2c exhibits the morphology of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ has the unique microstructural features of hollow interior cores. The cobalt cations begin to diffuse outwards, and the sulfur anions gradually migrate inward. As a result, the uniform-covered cobalt nanoparticles are vulcanized to be hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ nanoparticles [39]. Fig. 2f shows the NiO nanosheets are uniformly coated on $\phantom{+}C0_{9}S_{8}$ nanoparticles, consistent with the SEM results. Furthermore, the HRTEM image (Fig. 2g 2h) of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ show the obvious lattice distance of $0.126\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $0.146\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S1b), matching with the crystal plane of (31 1), and (22 0) of NiO, the above analysis results of HRTEM are well-matched with the corresponding XRD results. The elemental mapping of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ particles was conducted to examine the distribution of Co, S, Ni and O in $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ particles, and Fig. 2i 2l displays the EDS data, which demonstrate that the different elements are well dispersed and nanoparticles are packaged with continuous $\mathsf{N i}$ and O. The HRTEM analysis further indicate the coexistence of NiO and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and superior crystallinity of products.
At the same time, the surface chemical composition and status of elements were identified, with detailed results in Fig. 3a 3e. XPS full survey spectrum of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ is shown in Fig. 3e, and the element of Co, S, Ni and O could be detected. In the Co 2p spectra, Co 2p peaks observed the existence of $\mathtt{C o}2\mathtt{p}3/2$ and Co 2p1/2 electronic configuration followed with two satellite peaks at $785.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $801~\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to two spin–orbit doublets. Particularly, the fitting peaks centered at 781.6 eV, 797.0 eV and 778.6 eV, 794.8 eV were attributed to $C0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ , respectively [36,40]. Further, the $\textsf{S}2\mathsf{p}$ spectrum (Fig. 3b), peak appears at $161.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $162.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ were associated with S 2p3/2 and $S\mathsf{2p1}/2$ , respectively, and the satellites located at 166.7 eV [41]. Fig. 3c displays the Ni $2{\tt p}$ spectrum and the peaks at 853.9 and $871.90\ \mathrm{eV}$ were corresponded to $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ and Ni 2p1/2, respectively, and the peaks at 860.5 and $879.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to vibration satellite peaks [42]. As for the O1s spectrum, the characteristic peak at $529.4\mathrm{eV}$ belongs to the metal-oxygen bond [43]. From the results of EDS and XPS, it can be easily identified the successful formation of the production.
Fig. 3f describes the patterns of the as-obtained samples by powder XRD. For the characteristic diffraction peaks observed at
2h of $18.7^{\circ}$ , $25.5^{\circ}$ , $29.51^{\circ}$ , $31.1^{\circ}$ , $36.0^{\circ}$ , $46.6^{\circ}$ , $52.0^{\circ}$ , $55.5^{\circ}$ , and $62.2^{\circ}$ , can be indexed to the (200), (220), (311), (222), (40 0), (511), (440), (600), and (622) reflections, respectively, the data indicated that the diffraction peaks corresponded well with the reference pattern of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ (JCPDS-19-0364) [44,45]. The XRD pattern of pure NiO (Fig. S2) describes that the four main distinct peaks at values of $37.2^{\circ}$ , $43.1^{\circ}$ , $62.4^{\circ}$ and $75.0^{\circ}$ can be adequately related to the (111), (200), (220) and (311) plane of NiO (JCPDS-47-1049) [46]. As proved by the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and NiO in the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ , which confirms the complete conversion of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ , NiO and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{Ni}0$ . In addition, no evident residual diffraction peaks are detected, verifying the successful synthesis of the samples. The EDS spectrum (Fig. S3) of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ demonstrates the existence of elements Co, S, Ni and O, in agreement with the XRD results.
The BET surface areas and porous structures of the $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ were carried out by nitrogen $\left(\mathsf{N}_{2}\right)$ adsorption/desorption, and exhibit in Fig. 4, it is evident that the as-prepared samples present the type-Ⅳ isotherms [47]. Such isotherms mainly occur in the structure of porous material where the slits were caused by layered aggregation, as well as suggesting mesopores structures in $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ . $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ possessed a specific surface area of ${\sim}42.62~\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ which is larger than that of $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ $(26.50\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ samples. In the BJH pore size distribution curve (Fig. 4b), the products of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ and $\phantom{+}C09S_{8}$ endow with an average pore size of $16.69\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $20.47~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The results further confirm that the average pore size of the two samples were identity with mesopores range of $2\mathrm{-}50\ \mathrm{nm}$ As well as the mesoporous structure can increase the density of the active sites and the accessibility of the electrolyte for rapid ion transfer/diffusion.
3.2. Electrochemical performance evaluation of $C o_{9}S_{8}@N i O$ composite in three-electrode system
The electrochemical performances were initial investigated using a three-electrode system. Fig. 5a, S4a and S4c reveal the CV curves of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ , pure $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ and pure NiO electrodes in potential window range from 0 to $0.47~\mathrm{V}$ at scan rates between 2 and $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , respectively. Obviously, pairs of well-defined redox peaks were viewed in the CV curves at low scan rates, which were induced by the faradic capacitive behavior, and presenting the typical electrochemical behavior of redox-type electrode material. In detail, electrolyte ions can completely transport into almost all the available space of materials generating the adequate insertion reaction. The shapes of redox peaks in CV curves are not easy to be identified in high scan rates, which due to the momently and insufficient reaction between electrode material and electrolyte, and impairing the utilization ratio of the electrode material at the same time. It is clear that the oxidation peaks move towards positive potential and reduction peaks move towards negative potential, respectively. That may attribute to that charge transfer kinetics is the limiting step of the reaction [44]. And the whole results associated with reversible Faradaic redox reaction can be described as following:
$$
N i O+O H^{-}\leftrightarrow N i O O H+{\bf e}^{-}
$$
$$
C o_{9}S_{8}+O H^{-}\leftrightarrow C o_{9}S_{8}O H+e^{-}
$$
$$
C o_{9}S_{8}O H+O H^{-}\leftrightarrow H_{2}O+C o_{9}S_{8}O+e^{-}
$$
Fig. 5b implies the CV curves of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and NiO in $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}s^{-1}$ , and it can be clearly observed that $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ has a larger specific capacitance compared to $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and NiO. The results illustrate the fact that with the loading of NiO, a better electrical conductivity in the network and a high specific capacitance were achieved. Further, Fig. 5c exhibits the GCD curves of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ , the charge-discharge platforms are featured with the pseudocapacitance behavior, which indicating stable capacitance during charging and discharging. Fig. 5d describes the GCD curves of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and NiO at a current density of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , and the specific capacitance of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and NiO were 1627, 260 and $442.5\:\mathrm{F}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. As well as a higher capacitive property belonging to $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ can be observed obviously. When increased to a maximum of $20\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , the capacitance retention of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ , $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ and NiO maintains at $66.99\%$ $1090\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), $60.7\%$ $(157.8\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1})$ and $61.5\%(272.3\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , respectively. Furthermore, according to the calculation of formula (1), when the specific current is 1, 2, 5, 10, 12 and $20\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the specific capacitance of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ reaches 1627, 1596, 1555, 1234, 1134 and $1090\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , respectively. When the specific current is 1, 2, 5, 10. 12 and $20\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , the specific capacitance of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ is respectively 258, 252, 240.5, 223, 200.1 and $157.4\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as shown in Fig. S4b, and the specific capacitance of NiO is respectively 442.5, 408.5, 360.6, 317, 301.3 and $272.3\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ as shown in Fig. S4d. Meanwhile, the specific capacitance decreases gradually with the increase of current density that may attribute to the reaction between the active material and the electrolyte only remains on the outer surface of the as-prepared cathode at high current densities. Thus, the material embedded in the inner space makes little contribution to the capacitance.
Based on the above analysis, the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ endows with a higher specific capacitance, which can be due to the reasons as follows: on one side, the introduction of NiO is an important reason for the promising electrochemical performance and high specific capacitance of the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ composite. There are many nice electrical properties of NiO nanosheets. Here for example, NiO provides enough space for $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ during the reaction, shortening the length of $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ and facilitating the migration into the material. Meanwhile, the unique layered structure enlarged active sites, increased the contact chance between the active material and the electrolyte. On the other side, there existed synergetic effect between $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ and NiO, and its heterogeneous ‘‘core-shell” structure promotes the electrons and ions diffusion, which contributes to an excellent capacitance. What’s more, compared with other $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ -based electrode materials, such as S-rGO wrapped $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ nanocomposites, $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ nanoneedles, $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}@\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and so on [24,41,48,49], ${\sf C o}_{9}.$ $S_{8}@\mathsf{N i O}$ composites also show an outstanding reversible capacitance and rate capability.
To further explore the superior electrochemical performance of $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ , NiO and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ electrodes, the EIS tests were conducted and analyzed via the ZView with fitting lines and equivalent circuits as shown in Fig. 6a and 6b. The two figures depict that ${\sf C o}_{9}.$ $S_{8}@\mathsf{N i O}$ has a smaller semicircle diameter and charge transfer resistance than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and NiO, it can also be understood as the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ electrode has a faster charge transport rate, which reveals that the introduction of NiO can decrease the charge transfer resistance, and then increase the electrochemical reaction activity. Furthermore, comparing the tilt degree of the straight line with $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{Ni}0$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and NiO, we can conclude that $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ owns a slightly larger slope at the low-frequency region, revealing that NiO ensuring fully charge transfer at the electrolyte and electrode interface. Cycling performance is an important indicator for evaluating the quality of supercapacitors. Fig. 6d shows the repeatedly charging and discharging the capacitor at $5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ for over 6000 cycles. The NiO and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ electrodes kept relatively superior cycle stability. For $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ electrode, in the first 3000 cycles, the capacitance still remained at $92\%$ . At the last 3000 cycles, the capacitance of the initial capacitance retention reduced to $71.22\%$ , and degradation in the storage capacity was found. The possible reason is that the fast reversible redox reaction is not completely reversible in the actual reaction and the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ electrode material has occurred exhaustion of specific capacitance during the long cycles.
## 3.3. Performance evaluation of $C o_{9}S_{g}@N i O//A C$
To further investigate the application of the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ composites in the energy storage devices, an ASC $(\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}@\mathsf{N i O}//\mathsf{A C})$ was assembled by using $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ and activated carbon electrode as the positive and negative electrode, respectively, as illustrated in Fig. 7a. To explore the electrochemical potential window, the electrochemical performance of the AC electrode was also investigated (given in Fig. S5). The curves of CV and GCD are approximately rectangular and triangle, which indicating a typical EDLC characteristic of AC electrodes. The AC electrode conveys an effective specific capacity of $212\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}}$ at the specific current of $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and $158\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $20\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , indicating the AC electrode was an excellent candidate for negative material. Admittedly, the potential windows of anode and cathode electrodes are different, yet the asymmetric structure can fully use this value to extend the working voltage window and improve energy densities [50,51]. Fig. 7b delivers the CV curves for AC and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ at a scan rate of $5{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ . The curve of AC conveys a potential window from $^{-1}$ $\mathsf{V}$ to $0\mathrm{~V~}$ . Meanwhile, a couple of faradaic redox peaks can be achieved in a voltage window from 0 to $0.47~\mathrm{V}$ in the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ electrode. Thus, the ASC device can be worked in a high potential window of $1.5~\mathrm{V}$ without obvious polarization. The CV curves at different scan rates from 2 to a maximum of $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ and the voltage range from $0\mathrm{~V~}$ to $1.5~\mathrm{V}$ are explored in Fig. 7c. During the electrochemical analysis, the shape and redox peaks of the CV curves are similar, signifying a well-defined cyclic voltammetric behavior and pseudocapacitance behavior. Within the scope of low scan rate, a couple of redox peaks demonstrating the reversible redox processes. As expected, the potential window of the corresponding GCD curves could be extended to $1.5~\mathrm{V}.$ And the GCD curves of the ASC device at different current densities are shown in Fig. 7d. The specific capacitance of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}//\mathrm{AC}$ reaches 165.3, 153.3, 144.6, 143, 132, 124, and $114.6\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , corresponding with the specific current of 1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 12 and $20\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Symmetrical curves have remained. The specific capacity of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ //AC electrodes was shown in Fig. 7e and its capacitance retention up to $69.3\%$ .
The Ragone plots of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}//\mathrm{AC}$ ASC convey the relation between the energy density and power density (Fig. 7f). From the discharge curves, the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}//\mathrm{AC}$ exhibits a specific capacity of $165.3\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and the corresponding energy density of 51.65 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of 749.8 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ . Which is superior over than many asymmetric supercapacitors using similar $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ or NiO electrode reported previously (Table 1), such as $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{S}_{-\Gamma\mathrm{G}0//.}$ AC (39.51 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ 260 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [24], $\mathrm{Co_{9}S_{8}@N i C o_{2}O_{4}}//\mathrm{AC}$ (24.85 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ 222 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [49], NiO/NiS@CNT//AC (20.27 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ 8390 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [46], Ni-Co LDH//AC (17.5 Wh $\ k g^{-1}$ 10500 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [57], $\mathrm{NiO/Ni(OH)}_{2}{-}2//\mathrm{AC}$ (28.5 Wh $\ k g^{-1}$ $450~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}.$ ) [58], NC/Ni@NiO//AC (19.4 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , 700 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [59]. In addition, the long-term stability was often used to evaluate its cycle performance, the charging and discharging performance of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}//\mathrm{AC}$ was performed in 10,000 cycles (Fig. $7{\mathrm{g}}{\mathrm{.}}$ . It expresses a remarkable cycling performance of the ASC device with $81.79\%$ capacitance retention and the coulombic efficiency still can keep about $98\%$ after 10,000 cycles, suggesting that $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}//\mathrm{AC}$ is a promising candidate for supercapacitor and with the merit of excellent durability and outstanding electrochemical reversibility. To further evaluate the structural and compositional stability of the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ during long-term cycling stability testing, the XRD and SEM analyses were taken after 10,000 cycles. The XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ has not changed evidently before and after 10,000 cycles as shown in Fig. S6a. The presence of characteristic peaks suggests the original characteristic phase maintained. The
SEM image of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ after 10,000 cycles has been tested as shown in Fig. S6c. After long-term cycling, the overall morphology of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ is maintained, but the surface shows defects and small-scale ruptures, which is induced by redox reaction and constant volume shrinkage and expansion during the repetitive electrochemical cycles. Therefore, there was no obvious changes occurred in the structure and composition of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ during cycling.
In order to confirm the practical application value of ASC devices in real life, two ASC devices were connected in series. As expected, four red LEDs were lightened up and last for about
$30\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the process was accompanied by the continuous change of fluorescence intensity for the letter ‘‘NWPU” (Fig. 7h). This revealed a promising actual application value and an effective positive electrode for assembling next-generation highperformance supercapacitors based on the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ electrode.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, a rational nanostructure design and facile synthesis method by using ZIF-67 as a template were proposed to fabricate hierarchical $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO}$ electrode with outstanding pseudocapacitive performance. The integrated electrochemical properties benefit from the rational architecture between the self-supported $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ hollow structure and layered NiO. The unique structure enlarges active sites and promotes the diffusion of electrons and ions within the whole repetitive electrochemical cycles. The ideal architecture $(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{NiO})$ delivers exceptional specific capacitance as high as $1627\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $1.0\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , together with a long cycle lifetime of $71.22\%$ capacitance retained after 6000 cycles at $5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , this enhanced electrode outperforms many reported $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ based electrode materials. Further, a typical ASC device with stably lighting four light-emitting diodes, which exhibits an excellent energy density of 51.65 Wh $\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , and a maximum power density of 749.8 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , together with superior-excellent cycling durability with $81.79\%$ of the capacitance retention after 10,000 cycles. In light of these excellent properties, this work paves a way to develop high-performance energy storage in the future.
